{-
   `bitsUpTo`, `bitfield`, `gatherBits` and `scatterBits` - the operations BMI2 has an instruction
   for, **at the floor, where none of them does**.

   `Bits.bitsUpTo` is a member and `bitfield` is a plain function over it; `BitPermute` is a class of
   its own whose two methods are the two directions of an arbitrary bit permutation. All three
   classes are source (`lib/Core.yana`) and their integer instances are generated one instruction at
   a time (`defineBits` and `defineBitPermute` in resolve/intrinsic.cpp), so what this asserts is the
   whole path: the instance selection, the instruction, and the two completely different things the
   two targets reached here do with it.

   **There is no directive, so this is compiled for v2** - see test/directives.h, which is where the
   default is stated. That is deliberate and it is the more valuable of the two levels to assert:
   BMI2 is v3, so what the native run exercises here is `expandBitOperations`' own arithmetic - a
   shift, a mask and a guard for `bitsUpTo`, and the parallel-suffix network of five rounds at 32
   bits and six at 64 for the two permutations. BitPermute.Bmi2.yana beside this file is the same
   cases at v3, where each is one instruction. test/x64/BitOps.lower and test/x64/BitOpsBmi.lower are
   the byte listings of the two, and neither can say whether the answer is right.

   **A network is the kind of code a listing cannot check.** Every round of it is correct or the
   whole answer is wrong, and wrong by a permutation rather than by a bit - so a round with the wrong
   shift distance still answers something plausible for most masks and something wrong for the rest.
   The inverse identity in `inverse` is the case such a network cannot pass by luck: it holds for
   every mask, so a single bad round breaks it. Verified by giving one round the wrong distance,
   which this file caught and eight of its eleven groups did not.

   The JS run is the third answer, and it is a different algorithm again: a loop over the set bits of
   the mask, in the `number` domain below 33 bits and the `bigint` domain above.

   **The saturating count is the point of the second group.** `bitsUpTo` is total: a count at or
   above the width answers the value unchanged. No machine agrees about that on its own - x86's
   `bzhi` reads its count from a byte, so 256 is a count of zero there, and JS masks a shift count to
   five bits so `1 << 32` is 1 - and each backend pays for the difference in its own way. A target
   that dropped the guard fails here rather than in whatever program next computed a count it did not
   bound.

   Each case contributes a distinct power of two and `main` is 1073741823 when they all pass, so a
   failure names itself in binary.

   `@noinline` on the sources is what keeps the first cases from being answered by the constant fold
   instead. `folded` below is the opposite assertion - that the two paths agree.
-}

@noinline fn opaqueU32(x: U32) -> U32 = x
@noinline fn opaqueI32(x: I32) -> I32 = x
@noinline fn opaqueU64(x: U64) -> U64 = x
@noinline fn opaqueInt(x: Int) -> Int = x

fn check(condition: Bool, weight: Int) -> Int = if condition then weight else 0

-- The low bits kept. `0xdeadbeef` masked to sixteen is `0xbeef`, which is the field a caller would
-- otherwise have written a literal mask for.
fn low() -> Int =
    check(bitsUpTo(opaqueU32(3735928559), 16) == 48879, 1) +
    check(bitsUpTo(opaqueU32(3735928559), 4) == 15, 2) +
    check(bitsUpTo(opaqueU32(3735928559), 1) == 1, 4)

{-
   The two ends of the count, which is the whole of what the operation had to rule on.

   Zero keeps nothing. The width keeps everything, and so does *any* count above it - 40 and 200 are
   both "all of them" rather than 40 modulo 32 or 200 modulo 256. The three targets each need
   something different to make that true, and none of them needs it for a count in range.
-}
fn ends() -> Int =
    check(bitsUpTo(opaqueU32(3735928559), 0) == 0, 8) +
    check(bitsUpTo(opaqueU32(3735928559), 32) == 3735928559, 16) +
    check(bitsUpTo(opaqueU32(3735928559), 40) == 3735928559, 32) +

    {- The three counts that only `bzhi` can get wrong, sharing one weight because they are one
       assertion. 256 and 260 are the whole reason this line exists: the instruction reads its count
       from a *byte*, so their low bytes are 0 and 4 and an unsaturated `bzhi` answers 0 and 15 where
       the language answers everything. 200 fits in a byte and is above the width, so it is the case
       that passes either way and is here to say which of the two the others are about. -}
    check(bitsUpTo(opaqueU32(3735928559), 200) == 3735928559 &&
          bitsUpTo(opaqueU32(3735928559), 256) == 3735928559 &&
          bitsUpTo(opaqueU32(3735928559), 260) == 3735928559, 64)

-- 64 bits, where JS leaves the `number` domain entirely and every operation here is over a `bigint`.
fn wide() -> Int =
    check(bitsUpTo(opaqueU64(81985529216486895), 24) == 11259375, 128) +
    check(bitsUpTo(opaqueU64(81985529216486895), 64) == 81985529216486895, 256) +
    check(bitsUpTo(opaqueU64(81985529216486895), 0) == 0 &&
          bitsUpTo(opaqueU64(81985529216486895), 256) == 81985529216486895, 512)

-- The field extraction written over it. `0xdeadbeef`, eight bits from bit 16, is `0xad`.
fn field() -> Int =
    check(bitfield(opaqueU32(3735928559), start: 16, count: 8) == 173, 1024) +
    check(bitfield(opaqueU32(3735928559), start: 0, count: 16) == 48879, 2048) +
    check(bitfield(opaqueU32(3735928559), start: 28, count: 4) == 13, 4096)

{-
   The permutation, in both directions.

   `0x12345678` through `0xff00ff00` selects two bytes - `0x12` and `0x56` - and packs them adjacent
   lowest-first, which is `0x1256`. Depositing that back through the same mask puts each byte where
   it came from, which is the value masked and nothing else.
-}
fn permute() -> Int =
    check(gatherBits(opaqueU32(305419896), opaqueU32(4278255360)) == 4694, 8192) +
    check(scatterBits(opaqueU32(4694), opaqueU32(4278255360)) == 302011904, 16384) +
    check(gatherBits(opaqueU32(3735928559), opaqueU32(65535)) == 48879, 32768) +
    check(scatterBits(opaqueU32(15), opaqueU32(252645135)) == 15, 65536)

{-
   The identity the pair has: a deposit undoes an extract through the same mask, up to the bits the
   mask never named.

       scatterBits(gatherBits(v, m), m) == v and m

   Worth asserting on its own because it holds for *every* mask, so it is the one property a network
   that got one round wrong cannot pass by accident.
-}
fn inverse() -> Int:
    let value = opaqueU32(305419896)
    let mask = opaqueU32(4278255360)
    let wideValue = opaqueU64(81985529216486895)
    let wideMask = opaqueU64(71776123356119040)

    return check(scatterBits(gatherBits(value, mask), mask) == (value `and` mask), 131072) +
           check(scatterBits(gatherBits(wideValue, wideMask), wideMask) == (wideValue `and` wideMask), 262144)

-- The two degenerate masks. Nothing selected is nothing moved; everything selected is the value.
fn edges() -> Int =
    check(gatherBits(opaqueU32(3735928559), opaqueU32(0)) == 0, 524288) +
    check(scatterBits(opaqueU32(3735928559), opaqueU32(0)) == 0, 1048576) +
    check(gatherBits(opaqueU32(3735928559), opaqueU32(4294967295)) == 3735928559, 2097152) +
    check(scatterBits(opaqueU32(3735928559), opaqueU32(4294967295)) == 3735928559, 4194304)

{-
   A **signed** mask whose top bit is set, which is the operand a constant folder gets wrong.

   `-16777216` is `0xff000000` at `I32` and selects the top byte; `0x12345678`'s top byte is `0x12`,
   so the answer is 18. The trap is in how a constant of a signed type is *stored* - sign-extended,
   so the mask arrives with every bit above the width set as well - and a fold that walked those
   would iterate sixty-four times where the type has thirty-two bits and shift the answer off the
   end. Both spellings are here for that reason: `opaqueI32` keeps the operation at run time and the
   line below it hands the same pair to the folder.

   Sharing a weight because they are one assertion about one operand shape.
-}
fn signed() -> Int:
    let byteMask = opaqueI32(0 - 16777216)
    return check(gatherBits(opaqueI32(305419896), byteMask) == 18 &&
                 gatherBits(305419896 :: I32, (0 - 16777216) :: I32) == 18 &&
                 scatterBits(opaqueI32(18), byteMask) == 301989888 &&
                 gatherBits(opaqueI32(0 - 1), opaqueI32(0 - 1)) == (0 - 1), 8388608)

-- 64 bits again, where the mask has to be read as unsigned before a loop over its set bits can end.
fn widePermute() -> Int =
    check(gatherBits(opaqueU64(81985529216486895), opaqueU64(71776123356119040)) == 2329003 &&
          scatterBits(opaqueU64(291), opaqueU64(71776123356119040)) == 19070976, 16777216)

{-
   What the pair is actually for: two sixteen-bit numbers interleaved into one Morton code.

   One deposit each, through the even and the odd mask, and an `or`. Without the instruction this is
   the five-step shift-and-mask spread every graphics codebase carries a copy of; with it, it is the
   two lines below - and on a target that has neither, the compiler carries the spread instead of the
   program.
-}
fn morton(x: U32, y: U32) -> U32 =
    scatterBits(x, 1431655765) `or` scatterBits(y, 2863311530)

fn interleave() -> Int =
    check(morton(opaqueU32(43981), opaqueU32(4660)) == 1179474545, 33554432)

-- The constant path, which is the one group here that is *meant* to fold: the answer has to be the
-- one the instruction would have given.
fn folded() -> Int =
    check(bitsUpTo(3735928559 :: U32, 16) == 48879, 67108864) +
    check(bitfield(3735928559 :: U32, start: 16, count: 8) == 173, 134217728)

{-
   And the same operations reached through a *witness* rather than by name.

   A generated instance is a real function as well as an expansion at the call site, and this is the
   build that tells the two apart: a fixture with a `.run.expect` is compiled a second time with
   specialization declined, so each of these is then one body with the instance passed in, calling
   the class method through the table.
-}
@noinline fn (Bits(a)) lowVia(value: a, count: a) -> a = bitsUpTo(value, count)
@noinline fn (BitPermute(a)) gatherVia(value: a, mask: a) -> a = gatherBits(value, mask)

fn generic() -> Int =
    check(lowVia(opaqueU32(3735928559), 16) == 48879, 268435456) +
    check(gatherVia(opaqueU32(305419896), opaqueU32(4278255360)) == 4694, 536870912)

fn main() -> Int =
    low() + ends() + wide() + field() + permute() + inverse() + edges() + signed() + widePermute() +
    interleave() + folded() + generic()
